## ⚠️ Generating costs real money

Each clip spends roughly **4 credits** from the user's Ludo.ai balance.

**Never run a generating command unless the user asked for that specific generation**, and
confirm the scope first if they gave a budget or an ambiguous "do the rest". State the projected
cost (`clips × 4 credits`) before starting. `--dry-run` is free, needs no API key, and is always
the right first step.

`LUDO_API_KEY` lives in the gitignored `.env.local` and is loaded by the npm script itself. Never
commit it, never pass it as a CLI flag, and never rename it to `VITE_LUDO_API_KEY` — Vite inlines
every `VITE_`-prefixed variable into the client bundle, which would publish the key to anyone who
opens the game.

## The workflow

```bash
npm run sprites:emotions -- --dry-run --animal fox   # free: prints payloads, extracts references
npm run sprites:emotions -- --animal fox             # costs credits; writes to gitignored .ludo-review/
open .ludo-review/index.html                         # review every clip at stage scale
rm -rf .ludo-review/fox/angry                        # rejecting a clip = deleting its directory
npm run sprites:emotions -- --promote                # ship what survived
```

**The `--` is load-bearing.** `npm run sprites:emotions --dry-run` is not a dry run — npm eats
the flag, the script gets no arguments, and no arguments means the whole manifest: 30 clips,
~120 credits.

| Invocation | Cost |
|---|---|
| `-- --dry-run` | free, no API key needed |
| `-- --promote`, `-- --reindex` | free, no API call |
| `-- --animal X --emotion Y` | 4 credits per clip in scope |
| `-- --force …` | charges again — it exists to bypass the result cache |
| **no flags** | **the entire manifest (11 animals × 5 emotions), ~220 credits** |

## Names you will get wrong

Animal ids are the atlas names, not the species: `donkey-grey`, `white-sheep-1`, `brown-wolf`,
`fox`, `owl`, `raccoon`, `cow`, `cow-female-001`, `dog`, `mouse`, `pig`. Emotions: `talking`,
`doubtful`, `angry`, `thinking`, `sneaky`. Only the original six have generated clips; the
other five are imported but ungenerated — do not run a generating command for them unless
asked.

## Four things not to re-derive

- Playback rate is `frames / duration` — never hard-code one. A wrong rate is fixed by editing
  `promoted-clips.json` and running `-- --reindex`, free.
- `scripts/ludo/promoted-clips.json` is the source of truth; `--promote` merges into it. If the
  generated module loses an animal, look there first.
- `request_id` is an idempotency key: an unchanged rerun returns the previous result free.
- Never write a prompt from memory — SKILL.md's "Writing prompts" has seven numbered rules, a
  per-animal carrier table and the per-animal priors (the owl blinks and flares its wings, the
  sheep grazes, the wolf howls, the raccoon holds food). Ignoring them costs credits.

Generation and promotion are separate on purpose: diffusion output is not deterministic, so
nothing reaches `public/assets/` without a human having watched it loop.

## Where else to look

- `scripts/generate-emotion-sprites.mjs` — its header documents all six flags (`--dry-run`,
  `--animal`, `--emotion`, `--force`, `--promote`, `--reindex`) and the key handling.
- `scripts/ludo/emotion-manifest.json` — the prompts, with the authoring rules in its own
  `$comment` fields.
- `scripts/ludo/promoted-clips.json` — the committed record of every promoted clip. `--promote`
  merges into it; it is the source of truth for `src/phaser/animals/emotionSheets.generated.ts`.
- `docs/characters-and-animations.md` §9 — the runtime design (why clips are spritesheets and
  not atlases, and the scale/origin trap).

Never hand-edit `src/phaser/animals/emotionSheets.generated.ts`: its `scale` / `originX` /
`originY` are measured at promote time, and a clip without them renders at the wrong size. Fix
the measurement in `scripts/ludo/normalize.mjs` and re-promote instead.

